 :cutover/measured
 ;; What was actually run on 2026-09-08, as opposed to what the tranche
 ;; statuses above assert. A `:status :open` cannot distinguish "nothing has
 ;; happened" from "most of it works and here is the remainder", and T3 had
 ;; become the second while still reading as the first.
 ;;
 ;; Recorded as measurements with the command that produced them, not as status
 ;; flips: none of the four tranche statuses changed, because none was wrong.
 {:date "2026-09-09"
  :chicory-sweep-2026-09-09
  {:command "nbb --classpath '.:scripts/nbb_compat' scripts/verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover.cljs"
   :registered-checkouts 4780
   :files-scanned 39794
   :files-naming-chicory 25
   :buckets {:frozen 6 :mention-only 7 :test-oracle 3 :frozen-repo-other 7
             :outside-inventory 2}
   :problems 9
   :mention-only "A docstring is not a call site, and the rule is
    :new-chicory-call-sites :forbidden. Seven files name Chicory and import
    nothing: kotoba/src/kotoba/launcher.clj says `EXECUTE the module via
    kotoba.wasm-exec (com.dylibso.chicory)` in a docstring while requiring
    kotoba.wasm-exec, which IS the frozen entry; kototama's
    runtime_conformance.clj and five kotoba/mesh tests are the rest. Control,
    three ways, buckets summing to 25 each time: predicate live -> 7 / 3 / 7 /
    2 and problems 9; forced true -> mention-only 0 and problems 11, the
    previous number exactly; forced false -> mention-only 19 and problems 0,
    which is why the predicate is written to err toward REPORTING. A rule that
    can absorb every hit is one bad regex away from silencing the check.
    Known over-report: aiueos/src/aiueos/tcb.clj is prose too -- it spells the
    coordinate out to explain a maven path layout -- but the coordinate form
    is what the predicate looks for, so it stays in frozen-repo-other. Wrong
    toward reporting, deliberately."
   :note "The sweep used to report every non-frozen hit with one sentence, so a
    historical .clj test and a production provider's import sat in one
    undifferentiated pile of 19 -- while :cutover/rules already separates them
    (:historical-clj-tests :allowed-as-oracle-until-replaced beside
    :new-chicory-call-sites :forbidden). The verifier now classifies, the
    buckets are printed with their sum so a reclassification cannot lose a
    hit, and the test bucket is NAMED rather than merely excused: allowed
    UNTIL REPLACED is a clock, and a bucket nobody can see never runs down.
    Control, three ways: with the test predicate live, test-oracle 8 and
    problems 11; with both of its alternatives disabled, test-oracle 0,
    frozen-repo-other 14, outside-inventory 5 and problems 19 -- the old
    number, redistributed rather than suppressed; restored, 8 and 11 again.
    Buckets sum to 25 in all three."
   :frozen-repo-other-reachability
   "Measured 2026-09-09 by reading the (ns ...) form of every file in each
    repository rather than grepping the text -- a text search reported five
    requirers that turned out to be comments. NONE of the seven is on a
    production path. kototama's three providers are required only by
    src/kototama/host_parity_live.clj, whose own docstring says it `actually
    links and calls selected imports on the JVM tender (Chicory)` and whose
    only requirer is its own test; linker.clj, runtime_conformance.clj and
    clj/src/.../prior_shortcut_kotoba.clj are each required by one test and
    nothing else. So the whole kototama cluster is transitively test-only,
    which is the same disposition :historical-clj-tests already grants -- it
    simply does not live under test/. Whether that rule is about the path or
    about the reach is the owner's call and is not made here."
   :frozen-repo-other "Files that sit in a repo the inventory freezes at a path it
    does not: kototama/src/{linker,transport_provider,runtime_conformance,
    postgresql_pool_provider,postgresql_wire_provider}.clj, kototama/clj's own
    deps.edn and one source under it, aiueos/src/aiueos/tcb.clj, and
    kotoba/src/kotoba/launcher.clj. The inventory freezes IMPLEMENTATION
    PATHS, not repositories, so calling these brand-new call sites would
    overstate what was measured; they are reported under their own key."
   :outside-inventory "Two, unchanged since 2026-09-08 and now isolated from the
    other seventeen: provider-postgres/src/provider/postgres/pool.clj and
    provider-transport/src/provider/transport.clj. Whether the inventory
    absorbs them or they are removed remains the owner's decision and is still
    NOT made here. What changed is that the decision can be put to someone,
    which it could not be while those two were lines in a list of nineteen --
    and that four facts were measured for it, on 2026-09-09, which change what
    `post-freeze violation` means for these two."
   :outside-inventory-measured
   {:they-are-extractions-of-a-frozen-repos-files
    "provider-transport/src/provider/transport.clj differs from
     kototama/src/kototama/transport_provider.clj in 15 lines of 440, and every
     one is a namespace rename (kototama -> provider, including the keyword
     namespaces). provider-postgres/src/provider/postgres/pool.clj differs from
     kototama/src/kototama/postgresql_pool_provider.clj in 46 of 477. These are
     not newly written Chicory consumers; they are files that were split out of
     a repository the inventory freezes."
    :the-split-was-deliberate-and-is-documented-upstream
    "kototama/deps.edn says it in its own words: `not part of the runtime core.
     It lives in kotoba-lang/provider-postgres and its interop harness went
     with it. A deployment that wants it adds that dependency itself; the
     tender does not ship a database pool.` The extraction NARROWS the runtime
     core, which is the direction this cutover wants."
    :neither-declares-chicory
    "Zero Chicory coordinates in either deps.edn. Both depend on kototama --
     a frozen inventory entry that pins com.dylibso.chicory/{wasm,runtime} --
     so what Chicory they resolve, they resolve THROUGH the frozen entry. No
     new dependency was added by the split; a string moved."
    :the-transport-split-reduced-the-surface
    "kototama/src/kototama/transport_provider.clj imports
     `com.dylibso.chicory.runtime HostFunction` AND
     `com.dylibso.chicory.wasm.types ValType`. The extracted copy imports only
     ValType -- a type used to declare a host-function signature, not the
     runtime engine. The file that left the frozen repo names LESS Chicory than
     the one that stayed."
    :what-is-still-true
    "The sweep is right that the string appears outside the frozen paths, and
     :new-chicory-call-sites :forbidden is about call sites, not about
     dependency declarations. Nothing above says the rule was not crossed. It
     says the crossing is a documented extraction that reduced the runtime core
     and added no dependency -- which is the difference between `absorb these
     two into the inventory` and `remove them`, and that is the decision this
     record has always left to its owner."}}

