  :jvm-free-compiler
  {:verified-by
   "a PATH with no clojure and no java, plus a poison clojure script that logs
    every invocation -- so it-did-not-need-a-JVM is measured by the trace being
    empty, not by the command exiting 0"
   :jvm-free ["amu compile (wasm32 and all native ISA targets)"
              "amu check"
              "amu extract-native"
              "amu module-lock and multi-file compile --module-lock"]
   :still-jvm ["keygen" "sign" "measure-runtime" "trust-runtime" "run"
               "receipt" "verify-receipt" "sbom" "attest-release" "coverage"
               "inspect" "test" "package-aiueos-boot"
               "the aiueos kernel/UEFI packaging targets"]
   :ran "compiled native output executed under a from-source tools/kexe_loader.c: add(40,2) and a two-module double(21) both printed 42, with no JVM, no Node and no Wasm runtime"
   :honesty-gap
   "CLOSED, and it was never quite what this said. Re-measured 2026-09-09
    against amu 353a0677 with java/javac/clojure/clj stubbed to a deny-and-log
    script at the head of PATH, so the route is read off an empty trace rather
    than off an exit code:

      bin/amu    test|sbom|inspect              jvm=1   spawns, as asked
      bin/kotoba -M test|sbom|inspect           jvm=1   identical
      bin/amu    test|sbom --jvm-free           jvm=0   refuses, names why
      bin/kotoba -M test --jvm-free             jvm=0   refuses, names why
      bin/amu    check --jvm-free               jvm=0
      bin/kotoba -M check --jvm-free            jvm=0

    Neither half of the old sentence reproduces. bin/amu does NOT refuse a
    JVM-only command on its own: without --jvm-free it spawns, which is
    correct, because the caller did not ask. And bin/kotoba does not silently
    differ from it -- the two launchers behave the same, and both refuse out
    loud when --jvm-free is passed, each naming the command it will not run.
    So `fix the launcher before quoting any bin/kotoba result as JVM-free` no
    longer blocks T2: pass --jvm-free, and the empty trace is the evidence."}
  :tools-reader-exit
  {:note
   "org.clojure/tools.reader left amu's production classpath on this date
    (clojure -Spath | grep -c tools.reader, 1 -> 0). It was there only
    transitively via kotoba-sema, whose frontend read source with tools.reader
    on :clj and its own kotoba-reader on :cljs. kotoba-sema now uses one reader
    on both hosts.
    Collapsing that fork surfaced three defects that were live on the JVM-free
    runtime and invisible on the JVM: f32 decimal literals refused, 0x hex
    literals read as SYMBOLS (79 .kotoba files use them), and (def k 1.5)
    refused as not-closed. A fourth divergence was silent rather than a
    refusal: 0377 read as decimal 377 here and octal 255 under tools.reader."
   :adr "adr-2609081700-a-clj-test-of-a-cljc-compiler-tests-one-host"}
